7.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A remains the cleanest overall fit. The two identical voting-and-economic classes (A and B) plus the 50/50 issuance and two-director board deliver genuine parity of control without any extra parties on the board. Tyler’s direct Class A holding gives him straightforward dividend access, while Alex’s shares sit inside a separate corporation he co-owns with his wife; that structure lets him draw salary as an employee or officer without any forced dividend treatment on the operating company’s books. Class C is already authorized as a pure non-voting vehicle that can be issued to outside investors who will have zero say in operations—exactly what the partners asked for.  

C’s four-class design is unnecessary and creates its own problems: it forces Alex into a non-dividend class while still needing a holding-company workaround that isn’t actually present, and its Class C investor shares carry literally no economic rights at all, making them unattractive to any real outside capital. The extra classes also add drafting and compliance overhead with no functional benefit. Options that add extra directors (E) or reverse the dividend rights (B) break the equal-control or payment-preference requirements outright.  

A therefore satisfies every stated criterion with the least complexity and the most practical path for future passive investors.}

